WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.932
Cargo security fundamentals are a
repeatable engineering practice.

2
00:00:04.012 --> 00:00:06.805
Start by reviewing Cargo.toml and
Cargo.lock together.

3
00:00:06.885 --> 00:00:10.376
The manifest tells you what the project
intends to depend on and how it is

4
00:00:10.456 --> 00:00:11.095
configured.

5
00:00:11.175 --> 00:00:14.433
The lockfile tells you what versions Cargo
resolved for the build.

6
00:00:14.513 --> 00:00:17.364
Both views are necessary for real
dependency visibility.

7
00:00:17.444 --> 00:00:19.771
Know your direct and transitive
dependencies.

8
00:00:19.851 --> 00:00:22.935
Choose crates intentionally, understand
why they are needed,

9
00:00:23.015 --> 00:00:25.691
review their maintenance and compatibility
signals,

10
00:00:25.771 --> 00:00:28.563
and remove dependencies that no longer
earn their place.

11
00:00:28.643 --> 00:00:31.843
Update deliberately: do not ignore
dependency changes forever,

12
00:00:31.923 --> 00:00:34.832
but do not accept changes blindly without
tests and review.

13
00:00:34.912 --> 00:00:36.948
Review features and optional code paths.

14
00:00:37.028 --> 00:00:40.577
Default features, workspace feature
interactions, protocol support,

15
00:00:40.657 --> 00:00:42.577
parser formats, native integrations,

16
00:00:42.657 --> 00:00:46.089
and cryptographic backend choices can all
affect the final artifact.

17
00:00:46.169 --> 00:00:50.300
Select the minimum behavior required for
the job and revisit feature choices during

18
00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:51.427
dependency updates.

19
00:00:51.507 --> 00:00:54.357
Treat build scripts, procedural macros,
generated code,

20
00:00:54.437 --> 00:00:56.881
and native dependencies as trusted build
inputs.

21
00:00:56.961 --> 00:01:01.091
Protect registry credentials, private
package access, and publishing authority.

22
00:01:01.171 --> 00:01:04.836
Keep tokens out of repositories, logs,
history, and broad CI/CD exposure.

23
00:01:04.916 --> 00:01:07.651
Make publishing deliberate, reviewed, and
traceable.

24
00:01:07.731 --> 00:01:09.418
Finally, keep governance active.

25
00:01:09.498 --> 00:01:13.396
Use advisory, license, policy, and
SBOM-style checks to maintain visibility.

26
00:01:13.476 --> 00:01:15.628
Build through controlled CI/CD workflows.

27
00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:18.734
Keep artifacts traceable, logs clean,
releases reviewable,

28
00:01:18.814 --> 00:01:22.944
rollback expectations clear, and
dependency records ready for incident response.

29
00:01:23.024 --> 00:01:25.060
Cargo security is not a one-time cleanup;

30
00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:28.340
it is part of how Rust software is
responsibly built and released.
